@title Two Minutes at the Poster
@kind paper
@source paper:Obfuscation as a Probe of Human and Machine Code Comprehension
@cite Jack Le (2026). Do Machines Struggle Where Humans Do? Obfuscation as a Probe of Human and Machine Code Comprehension. In Companion Proceedings of the 2026 ACM SIGPLAN International Conference on Systems, Programming, Languages, and Applications: Software for Humanity (SPLASH Companion '26), Oakland, CA, USA. ACM, 2 pages. doi:10.1145/3837729.3839573. ACM Student Research Competition, SPLASH/ISSTA.
@link ../../assets/documents/publications/2026SPLASH-SRC.pdf Two-page SRC abstract
@link https://doi.org/10.1145/3837729.3839573 DOI
@authors Jack V. Le
@note Dialogue is dramatized. The Judge is an abstract role, not a real competition judge, and the two-minute clock is the story's device. Figures marked § are quoted from the two-page SRC abstract; the human and model studies it summarizes are joint work, credited in their own stories.
@status draft
@verify
@palette moss
@include _facts/obfuscation.vn
@fact n_questions = 12 ^§3.2
@fact snips_per_lang = 10 ^§3.1
@fact models_a = seven ^§3.3
@fact models_b = nine ^§3.3
@fact rho_bands = mean ρ ≈ 0.24 to 0.30 ^§4
@cast Jack hue=210 glasses
@cast Judge name="the Judge" hue=350 bun
@cast You player hue=120

@bg lecture
@show Jack left
One easel, one printout, a hall full of both. The poster session.
@show You right
You: Poster is up. Who is the one with the clipboard?
Jack: A judge. They walk the row, stop, and you get two minutes before they move on.
@show Judge center
Judge: Two minutes. Title first.
Jack: Do Machines Struggle Where Humans Do? Obfuscation as a probe of human and machine code comprehension. ^p.1
Jack: Code obfuscation targets human comprehension, but current evaluations primarily measure language model performance degradation
  instead of whether models fail in the same way as humans. ^§abs
Judge (puzzled): So the field has been measuring the wrong thing.
Jack: Obfuscation is almost always measured without people, on two untested assumptions: that difficulty is monotonic,
  and that what burdens a person burdens an automated analyst, now usually an LLM. ^§1
Jack: If models fail where humans do, obfuscated code benchmarks human-like reasoning; if not, strong scores mask brittle competence. ^§1
@card Five tiers, each verified execution-equivalent on the study's inputs | L0: the original function with its name masked | L1: uninformative renaming; short, disjoint identifiers force structural reasoning | L1b: adversarial renaming; misleading names drawn from one semantic domain, a Stroop-like trap | L2: control-flow flattening; dispatchers and indirect jumps obscure execution order | L3: both, an upper bound on difficulty ^§2.1
Judge: And the people?
Jack: {n_humans} undergraduate CS majors, a supervised {session_min}-minute session of {n_questions} questions, output prediction scored by exact match against execution:
  {n_answers} responses with paired correctness and response time. ^§3.2
Jack: {n_models} LLMs on the same tiers, {n_runs} runs in all. {models_a} enter the human comparison on the human-study set, {models_b} on a disjoint, model-only set. ^§3.3
Judge (deadpan): One minute.
@thumb
Judge: You have one result's worth of my attention left. Where do you start?
== hub
* (once) The correlation. Whether a model's difficulty tracks a person's. -> lead_corr
* (once) Python. The surprise. -> lead_python
* (once) The Block Model. Where exactly it breaks. -> lead_block
* Thank the judge and read the four contributions as printed. -> pitch

== lead_corr
@if lead -> corr_say
@set lead = corr
== corr_say
Jack: Then I open with the comparison itself. Not whether obfuscation hurts a model, but whether a model's difficulty tracks a person's, task by task.
Judge (thinking): Task by task. Most people show me an average.
You: Keep going, or pick another lead?
-> hub

== lead_python
@if lead -> python_say
@set lead = python
== python_say
Jack: Then I open with Python, because it is the tier that refuses to do what the field assumes.
Judge (surprised): Which assumption?
Jack (smile): Monotonic difficulty. It is on the board.
-> hub

== lead_block
@if lead -> block_say
@set lead = block
== block_say
Jack: Then I open with where it breaks. Not that a model fails, but at which level: atoms, blocks, relations, or macro structure.
Judge: Name the level when we get there.
-> hub

== pitch
@hide You
@show Jack left (neutral)
@show Judge right (neutral)
Judge: Time. Read me the contributions as printed, then I will ask about your lead.
@card Contributions | 1: The first per-task human–model difficulty correlation, localized via the Block Model. | 2: Evidence against monotonic difficulty: a reproducible Python renaming effect. | 3: Only reasoning-tuned models track human difficulty. | 4: Language-specific behavioral reporting of obfuscation strength. ^§4
Jack: Difficulty is non-monotonic and language-specific. Human accuracy falls from L0 at {human_l0} to L3 at {human_l3}:
  JavaScript declines steadily, {js_l0} to {js_l3}, flattening hurting less than renaming,
  whereas Python rises under renaming, {py_l0} to {py_rename}, L3 exceeding L2. ^§4
@if lead == python -> react_python
-> effort

== react_python
Judge (surprised): There is your lead. A language that gets easier.
Jack: Under renaming. And measured with people, which is the part that was missing.
-> effort

== effort
Jack: Effort matches the dual-process account: response time jumps by {rt_jump} once code is obfuscated, then plateaus;
  errors cluster at time extremes, and correct L1b answers are slower than incorrect ones. ^§4
Jack: Reasoning-tuned models align with humans on difficulty, not language. They correlate with human task-level difficulty, Spearman {rho_reason};
  coder- and instruction-tuned models do not, {rho_other}, regardless of size, so the most accurate model is not the most human-like. ^§4
@if lead == corr -> react_corr
-> bands

== react_corr
Judge (thinking): Your lead. Per task, not on average. Does it survive experience?
Jack: It does. Next line.
-> bands

== bands
Jack: Alignment holds across all three experience bands, {rho_bands}, with no experience-by-alignment interaction in a mixed model.
  But no model reproduces the experts' gain under adversarial renaming or the Python renaming gain: alignment reflects shared difficulty, not identical mechanism. ^§4
Jack: Failures localize by Block Model level. Chain-of-thought length correlates negatively with accuracy, {rho_cot};
  control-flow failure tracks dispatcher-state complexity, the relational level;
  adversarial renaming acts non-additively at the atom level: displacement or perplexity spikes alone barely move accuracy, but together collapse it to {adv_collapse}. ^§4
Jack: The sharpest probe is a Stroop-like trap: a variable named `_lastNSecs` that tracks recursion depth baits a fast, confident, wrong answer. ^§2.2
@if lead == block -> react_block
-> close

== react_block
Judge: There is your level. Atoms.
Jack (smile): Atoms.
-> close

== close
Jack: This alignment, rather than raw accuracy, distinguishes robust comprehension from brittle pattern matching. ^§abs
Judge (deadpan): That was two minutes. Thank you.
@hide Judge
@show You right
You: Well?
Jack (smile): Received in June, accepted in July. ^p.2
@end
